    {
      "sectionId": "section-disclaimer",
      "heading": "Understanding RPGBOT’s Rating System",
      "sourceAnchor": "disclaimer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-disclaimer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "RPGBOT uses a color coding scheme which is simple to understand and easy to read at a glance. This system is designed to help players make informed decisions for their dnd character builds."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-disclaimer-2",
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Red"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Bad, useless options, or options which\n\t\tare extremely situational. Nearly never useful."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Orange"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": OK options, or useful options\n\t\tthat only apply in rare circumstances. Useful sometimes."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Green"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Good options. Useful often."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Blue"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Fantastic options, often essential\n\t\tto the function of your character. Useful very frequently."
              }
            ]
          ],
          "depths": [
            0,
            0,
            0,
            0
          ],
          "itemKinds": [
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered"
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-disclaimer-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The advice offered below is based on the current "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "State of the 2014 DnD 5e Character Optimization Meta",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/dnd5/characters/state-of-the-meta/"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " as of when the article was last updated. Keep in mind that the state of the meta periodically changes as new source materials are released, and the article will be updated accordingly as time allows."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-disclaimer-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "We will not include 3rd-party content, Unearthed Arcnaa content, or homebrew in guides for official content because we can’t assume that your game will allow them."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-warforged-traits",
      "heading": "Warforged Traits",
      "sourceAnchor": "warforged-traits",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-warforged-traits-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Warforged’s traits are broadly useful, consistently benefit characters of any class."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-warforged-traits-2",
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Ability Score Increase"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Borderline perfect\n\t\tfor most classes even without the custom origin rules."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Size"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Medium. Rarely impactful."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Speed"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Standard."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Constructed Resilience"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Broadly useful\n\t\tdefenses. Excellent on any character."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Sentry’s Rest"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Not especially impactful in\n\t\tmost games, but helpful for keeping watch."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Integrated Protection"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Not flashy, but\n\t\tconsistently impactful on almost any character."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Specialized Design"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Excellent on any\n\t\tcharacter. If you need a default, take Perception and Chef’s Tools for the\n\t\tbonus Short Rest healing."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Languages"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Common plus one is\n\t\tstandard."
              }
            ]
          ],
          "depths": [
            0,
            0,
            0,
            0,
            0,
            0,
            0,
            0
          ],
          "itemKinds": [
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered"
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-warforged-traits-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Warforged, despite their appearance, are not constructs. In 3rd edition when they were first introduced to DnD, warforged were constructs, but in 5e they are humanoids. It’s not entirely clear why this is the case, but at the time that Eberron: Rising from the Last War was published, there had not yet been any non-humanoid playable races."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Warforged Classes",
      "sourceAnchor": "classes",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classes-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Warforged are exceptionally versatile, second in versatility only to the Variant Human. Constitution is a great option for literally every class, few classes benefit significantly from more than one flexible ability increase, and a bonus AC is literally always useful."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classes-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Because the custom origin rules change things so little for the Warforged, the custom origin rules are not addressed  in a separate section as I have done in most of my race handbooks."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-artificer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Artificer",
      "sourceAnchor": "artificer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-artificer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "An obvious choice. The Warforged’s Constitution increase and AC bonus are\n\tgreat on top of the Artificer’s already exceptional durability. Since the\n\tArtificer needs little beyond Intelligence, the single flexible ability\n\tincrease works great. If you build a Battlesmith you can be a robot with a pet\n\trobot, and if that doesn’t sound like fun I feel like you and I have very\n\tdifferent ideas of fun."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-barbarian",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Barbarian",
      "sourceAnchor": "barbarian",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-barbarian-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Increase your Strength, grab some medium armor, and you’re borderline\n\tunstoppable. Resistance to poison and immunity to both disease and sleep\n\tmitigate or negate some common challenges which front-line melee characters\n\tface, adding to the Barbarian’s incredible durability. The Warforged may even\n\tunseat the Mountain Dwarf as the hardest barbarian to kill."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-bard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Bard",
      "sourceAnchor": "bard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-bard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "More AC makes options like College of Valor and College of Swords more\n\tdurable, which is great if you’re trying to play a spellcaster in melee with\n\td8 hit points. Spellcasting is still going to be more effective than using\n\tweapons most of the time, but you can stand in melee with a shield in one hand\n\tand a one-handed instrument like a tamborine in the other and do just fine."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-cleric",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Cleric",
      "sourceAnchor": "cleric",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-cleric-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Boost your Wisdom and you’re the cleric with the world’s highest AC. Put your\n\tfree skill proficiency into Perception to capitalize on your high Wisdom and\n\tyour ability to remain conscious while taking a long rest."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-druid",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Druid",
      "sourceAnchor": "druid",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-druid-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Druids have notoriously poor AC, and while you lose most racial traits while\n\tusing Wild Shape, any circle which isn’t dependent on Wild Shape is a great\n\toption. Warforged work especially well for Circle of Spores thanks to their\n\tConstitution increase and bonus AC. Put your free skill proficiency into\n\tPerception to capitalize on your high Wisdom and your ability to remain\n\tconscious while taking a long rest."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-fighter",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Fighter",
      "sourceAnchor": "fighter",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-fighter-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Fighter is one of the simplest classes, and the Warforged is one of the\n\tsimplest races despite their built-in decision points. Put them together, and\n\tyou can deeply customize your build to fit your play style, confident that\n\tyour character will durable and effective."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-monk",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Monk",
      "sourceAnchor": "monk",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-monk-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Monks typically need increases to both Dexterity and Wisdom so that they can start with an AC high enough to survive their d8 hit points. The Warforged addresses this nicely with the bonus AC, and the Constitution increase mitigates the low hit dice. Unfortunately, the Warforged’s resistance to poison and immunity to disease both become redundant, but if you survive to the levels where that happens I think you’ll be fine with the redundancy."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-monk-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "If you use the custom origin rules, you can move the increases to Dexterity and Wisdom. Starting with 16 in each gives you an AC of 17, higher than any other monk can achieve at first level with the exception of the Tortle."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-paladin",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Paladin",
      "sourceAnchor": "paladin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-paladin-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "You’ll be crazy durable, and with the extra AC it’s safer to opt for a two-handed weapon. Or you can go for a shield and be an impenetrable tank."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-ranger",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Ranger",
      "sourceAnchor": "ranger",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-ranger-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Extra durability, and the extra skill proficiency helps close the gap in\n\tskills between the Ranger and the Rogue."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-rogue",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Rogue",
      "sourceAnchor": "rogue",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-rogue-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "While the Warforged’s bonus AC isn’t as import for the Rogue thanks to their\n\tability to remove themselves from danger and options like Uncanny Dodge, the\n\textra durability is great insurance for a class which often finds themselves\n\tin dangeorus places."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-sorcerer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Sorcerer",
      "sourceAnchor": "sorcerer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-sorcerer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The idea of a bloodline is a bit silly for a race with no blood or ancestry,\n\tbut if your DM is willing to ignore that strangeness you have some options.\n\tDraconic bloodline sorcerer will get you 14+Dex AC, which is unusually high\n\tfor similar spellcasters and combined with options like Shield you’ll be\n\treasonably durable even when you draw a lot of attacks."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-warlock",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Warlock",
      "sourceAnchor": "warlock",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-warlock-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Warforged is a great option for the Hexblade, making you much more durable than your d8 hit dice would indicate. With extra durability, you’re much more able to handle running around in melee with a weapon for extended periods."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-wizard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Wizard",
      "sourceAnchor": "wizard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-wizard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Wizard may be the worst option for the Warforged, but only because the\n\tWizard spends so much time avoiding the problems which the Warforged is built\n\tto endure. If you’re taking a bunch of damage or being targeted by effects to\n\twhich the Warforged is resistant r immune, you should be invisible, running,\n\tor otherwise magically countering the effects. Still, Constitution and\n\tIntelligence are really all that the Wizard needs, so you’re fine if you never\n\tsee poison or disease come up."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
